  "codebook": {
    "clarification": "Include both explicit first-person claims ('I helped secure,' 'my amendment,' 'legislation I authored,' 'this follows my appeal/request/letter') and implicit claims in which the senator's office announces federal money or a favorable federal decision benefiting the state — the announcement itself functions as a credit claim even without the words 'I secured.' Joint announcements with other delegation members or co-sponsors count. Claims about the senator's own legislation count even if the bill is only introduced or pending, because the senator is claiming responsibility for the initiative; this includes announcements of introduced, sponsored, or cosponsored bills, adopted amendments, included provisions, committee or floor passage, and negotiated agreements folded into legislation. Also count (a) claims that an agency acted in response to the senator's request, letter, nomination, or pressure, and (b) accomplishment claims embedded in a release nominally about something else (e.g., touting an enacted bill inside an essay-contest release).",
    "definition": "Credit-claiming occurs when a senator presents himself or herself (alone or jointly with delegation colleagues) as personally responsible, in whole or in part, for a government action or outcome portrayed as desirable. Typical forms: announcing federal funds, grants, loans, projects, designations, military missions, appointments, or favorable agency decisions benefiting the senator's state; introducing, sponsoring, cosponsoring, amending, advancing, or passing one's own legislation and touting it; and claiming results (e.g., recoveries, reforms, concessions) from one's oversight, negotiations, or advocacy.",
    "negative_clarification": "Do NOT code as credit-claiming: (a) pure position-taking — explaining votes, supporting or opposing others' bills or nominations, reacting to external events, or criticizing the administration, other parties, or foreign actors, no matter how forceful; (b) advocacy in progress with no delivered outcome — letters, requests, meetings, hearings, questions to officials, demands, and pledges to keep fighting, unless the release also claims an accomplished result or the senator's own enacted/advanced legislative action; (c) ceremonial or commemorative content — condolences, memorials, honoring constituents or former officials, holiday remembrances, award ceremonies, essay contests, and event announcements; (d) crediting other actors (the President, a state board, an advisory panel, TVA, colleagues) without claiming a personal causal role; and (e) vague or collective references — party-level claims ('Republican policies worked'), general leadership labels, an interest-group award for one's voting record, or 'played a key role in a debate' — where no specific delivered benefit or legislative product is claimed.",
    "negative_examples": [
      {
        "document_id": "656",
        "explanation": "Kerry blasts the administration's 'incompetence' on port security and the Dubai Ports deal without claiming any personal role in the deal's collapse; attacking an opponent's record is position-taking, not credit-claiming."
      },
      {
        "document_id": "2675",
        "explanation": "Dodd, Lieberman, and Simmons describe meetings urging BRAC commissioners to spare Sub Base New London; they claim effort and advocacy ('our work isn't done') but no achieved outcome, so there is nothing yet to claim credit for."
      },
      {
        "document_id": "3534",
        "explanation": "Domenici 'credited President Bush' with a plan on gasoline prices; attributing the desirable action to someone else while claiming no personal role is the mirror image of credit-claiming."
      }
    ],
    "positive_examples": [
